    Simulation duration — unstable case first. Run the exact same unstable case for 10, 100, 500, and 1000 steps. Look mainly at velocity_z and temperature. This answers the question you already encountered: when does the initially straight flow actually begin to develop visible turbulent/convective structures?

    Stable surface cooling strength. Keep the stable case identical and change only:
    ABL.surface_temp_rate = -0.10, -0.25 (original), -0.50.
    Compare temperature and velocity_z. You should see stronger cooling produce stronger stable stratification and more suppression of vertical mixing.

    Unstable surface heating strength. Keep the unstable case identical and use:
    0.5 × original flux, 1 × original flux, 2 × original flux.
    Don’t invent a new physical value yet; scaling the existing test value is better for learning. Compare velocity_z, temperature, and eventually TKE. You want to see whether stronger surface heating leads to stronger convection.

    Surface roughness. Use one case, preferably your neutral abl_bds, and vary only ABL.surface_roughness_z0. For example, compare 0.015, 0.15 (original), and 0.30 m. Look at horizontal velocity versus height. This teaches you directly how surface drag produces wind shear. These are learning experiments, not proposed ocean roughness values.

    Wind speed. Your original wind magnitude is about 8 m/s. Try roughly 4, 8, and 12 m/s, keeping the same direction. Compare the velocity profile and turbulence. This lets you see how mechanical/shear-driven turbulence changes independently of thermal stability.

    ABL / inversion height. Start from abl_bds. Your original profile begins its inversion at about 650 m. Create one case with a lower inversion and one with a higher inversion, while keeping the temperature difference similar. In ParaView you should literally see the neutral mixed layer becoming shallower or deeper.

    Inversion strength. Keep the heights fixed and change the temperature jump. For example, instead of the original 300 → 308 K, try a weaker and a stronger jump. This teaches you how strongly the capping inversion confines the ABL.

    Temperature stability without changing wind. Create otherwise identical cases with a neutral, stable, and unstable thermal setup. This is particularly useful because the existing Kynema examples change several other things too. Once you do this, differences in velocity_z are much easier to attribute specifically to stability.

    First mesh experiment. Once you're comfortable with those parameters, keep one physical case completely fixed and change only the mesh: for example 32³, 48³, 64³ for the same domain. Compare temperature structure, velocity fluctuations, and computational time. This begins the “create your own mesh” part your supervisor mentioned.

    First custom domain. Finally, define your own geometry.prob_hi and amr.n_cell together. At that point you should calculate the resulting Δx, Δy, and Δz yourself and decide whether they are appropriate for the ABL structures you want to resolve.

